From db13687a781898a979a97a220a656b9230706a73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:33:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: the tile mask of what is drawn over a picture, to
 the CPU without waiting

Pass 7 of the source packer makes the tiles of the raster (16 x 16 pixels, 240 x
135) that the composite of Kodi's GUI can draw on: every tile where a sample of
the GUI texture, with the bilinear margin, can have an alpha above 0. The CPU
never saw it. dvbridge_source_tiles_request() copies it (RGBA and unsigned bytes,
which every implementation reads from an 8-bit target, the flag in red) into a
pixel pack buffer with a fence, as dvbridge_verify_compare() does, and
dvbridge_source_tiles_poll() takes it back when the fence is signalled, without
waiting; GL state is saved and restored around the read. The request is refused
where the last picture has no tiles (not made, or no GUI told) or one is waiting.

dvbridge_tiles.h merges a mask into the fewest rectangles a row-run join gives
(at most 24; -1 where there are more, or the marked tiles are over a share of the
raster), and says why drawing the composite's quad under glScissor for each is
the same bytes as the whole quad. CDVBridgeGLES gets RequestDrawnTiles() and
PollDrawnTiles(). Nothing uses them yet.

Host tests (outside the tree): tiles_merge_test (random masks, exact cover, the
limits; a mutant that joins rows of different width fails).
